   "source": [
    "# Write your code here\n",
    "r=0.95#if not it doesn't give a result\n",
    "a0=1\n",
    "a1=-r\n",
    "b0=1\n",
    "b1=1\n",
    "ww = np.arange(-np.pi, np.pi, np.pi/500)\n",
    "\n",
    "_, HH = signal.freqz([b0,b1], [a0,a1], ww)\n",
    "\n",
    "plot_frequency_response(ww, HH)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "BNBnVE8UMh32"
   },
   "source": [
    "1.4. Change the parameter $r$ in the range $(0, 1)$. What happens when $r$ is increased?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "QBB8-aCfNLaZ"
   },
   "source": []
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The closer r is to 0 the more smooth the slope of the magnitude is, and the phase changes at 0 radians smoother (more inclination, less sudden and more like a single line), the more r comes closer to 1 the more magnitude is reached at the pieck (at 0 radians), and therefore is more excentuated, and for th pgase, the change from poitive to negative through the 0 value os more sudden.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "7Julmw_jNQNc"
   },
   "source": [
    "1.5 What type of filter is (low-pass, band-pass, or high-pass filter)?\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "nC6TEgMdNof6"
   },
   "source": [
    "Low pass filter, since it excentuates the low frequencies (closer to 0) and tries to smooth down the ones more away from it (closer to pi or so).\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "fuxzcurPNrDd"
   },
   "source": [
    "1.6 Now we can use this IIR filter to extract the envelope. Let's define a new function `envelope_iir(x)` that applies the filter to the absolute value of the signal. Note that we are using signal.lfilter function to apply the filter.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "M8QlPifSOZQ_"
   },
   "source": [
    "Extract the envelope of your reference signal using both the FIR and IIR filters and compare the results plot them along with the signal. Change the value of $r$ until you are comfortable with the result."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "TIR7xGFlO2ug"
   },
   "source": [
    "1.7 What are the main differences between the two filters? For instance, compare the number of coefficients."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "epowKhs1PAPt"
   },
   "source": []
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "N-i3J_MkPnSc"
   },
   "source": [
    "1.8 Apply the new envelope (extracted from the IIR filter) to the synthesis generated with the `synthesize` function and listen to the result."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "LZAxSl7xP0zZ"
   },
   "source": [
    "---"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "7Bg2sbL9aGaL"
   },
   "source": [
    "**2. Band-pass filters**"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "5fzaFxPOdTtW"
   },
   "source": [
    "Now let's work with the following system: \n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "H(z) = \\frac{(1-z^{-1})(1+z^{-1})}{(1-re^{j\\omega_n}z^{-1})(1-re^{-j\\omega_n}z^{-1})}, \\quad 0<r<1\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Note that the poles are $p_1 = re^{j\\omega_n}$ and $p_2 = re^{-j\\omega_n}$; and the zeros are $z_1=1$ and $z_2=-1$. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "BFHMIXUDeBM7"
   },
   "source": [
    "2.1 Use the function `plot_zeros_poles` to plot the zeros and poles of the system.\n",
    "\n",
    "Note: use $w_n = \\pi/4$ and $r=0.99$."
   ]
  },
